proven wrong or significantly revised in the past as new evidence came to light.\n\n Here are some notable historical examples:\n\nGeocentric Model vs. Heliocentric Model:\nSettled Science: For centuries, the Ptolemaic geocentric model, where Earth was at the center of the universe with celestial bodies revolving around it, was considered settled.\n\nRevision: Nicolaus Copernicus proposed the heliocentric model, which was later supported by observations from Galileo Galilei and Johannes Kepler, fundamentally changing our understanding of the solar system.\n\nPhlogiston Theory:\nSettled Science: In the 17th and 18th centuries, phlogiston theory was widely accepted to explain combustion, suggesting that phlogiston was released when something burned.\n\nRevision: Antoine Lavoisier's experiments led to the discovery of oxygen and the understanding of oxidation, debunking phlogiston theory.\n\nLuminiferous Aether:\nSettled Science: Before the early 20th century, many scientists believed in the existence of aether, a medium through which light waves were thought to travel.\nRevision: The Michelson-Morley experiment failed to detect this aether, paving the way for Einstein's theory of special relativity, which does not require an aether for light propagation.\n\nSteady State Theory vs. Big Bang Theory:\nSettled Science: The steady-state theory, which proposed an unchanging universe with new matter being continuously created, was considered a competing theory to the Big Bang.\nRevision: Observations of the cosmic microwave background radiation and galaxy distributions led to the widespread acceptance of the Big Bang theory.\nSpontaneous Generation:\n\nSettled Science: It was once believed that life could arise from nonliving matter, like maggots from rotting meat or mice from straw.\nRevision: Louis Pasteur's experiments in the 19th century demonstrated that life comes from pre-existing life, disproving spontaneous generation.", 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [],